"""Original KS3 retrieval questions and definition cards, keyed to the taught concepts."""
R={}
def pack(key,cards,q,correct,wrong1,wrong2,why):
 R[key]={'cards':[dict(term=t.split('=',1)[0],definition=t.split('=',1)[1]) for t in cards.split('~')], 'quiz':dict(q=q,opts=[correct,wrong1,wrong2],a=0,why=why)}

pack('safety','Hazard=Something with the potential to cause harm.~Risk=The likelihood and seriousness of harm occurring.','Which is a specific control for a splash hazard?','Wear eye protection.','Simply write “be careful”.','Remove all measurements.','A control should state an action that reduces the identified risk.')
pack('variables','Independent variable=The factor deliberately changed.~Dependent variable=The factor measured or observed.~Control variable=A factor kept consistent to make a comparison fair.','When testing temperature against dissolving time, what is measured?','Time taken to dissolve.','The temperature chosen.','The label on the beaker.','The dependent variable is the measured outcome, not the setting you change.')
pack('planning','Resolution=The smallest change an instrument can detect.~Repeatability=Agreement when the same person repeats a method with the same equipment.','Why time ten swings instead of just one?','Reaction time has a smaller fractional effect.','It removes all systematic errors.','It makes the clock perfectly accurate.','The timing interval is longer, so the same reaction-time delay is a smaller fraction of it.')
pack('burner','Combustion=A chemical reaction with oxygen that transfers energy to the surroundings.~Safety flame=The visible yellow Bunsen flame used when not actively heating.','Opening a Bunsen air hole mainly changes what?','The air and oxygen supply.','The identity of the gas fuel.','The mass of the metal barrel.','Admitting more air supports more complete combustion and produces the blue heating flame.')
pack('data','Continuous data=Measurements that can take any value within a range.~Discrete data=Counts that take separate values.~Conclusion=An answer to an investigation question supported by evidence.','What supports a strong conclusion?','A pattern backed by numerical evidence.','A claim based on one favourite result.','A graph with no labelled axes.','Evidence should link the observed relationship to the investigation question.')
pack('evaluate','Random error=Unpredictable variation between measurements.~Systematic error=A consistent measurement bias in one direction.~Anomaly=A result that does not fit the overall pattern.','A balance always reads 2 g too high. What should be done?','Correct the zero or calibration error.','Average more readings without correction.','Call it random variation.','Repeating measurements retains a consistent offset; calibration is needed.')
pack('microscope','Magnification=Image size divided by actual size, using the same length units.~Resolution=The ability to distinguish nearby details as separate.','A ×10 eyepiece and ×40 objective give what magnification?','×400.','×50.','×4.','Multiply the two lens magnifications: 10 × 40 = 400.')
pack('cell','Nucleus=A cell structure containing genetic material.~Cell membrane=The boundary that controls exchange into and out of a cell.~Chloroplast=A structure containing chlorophyll for photosynthesis.','Which statement about plant cells is accurate?','Root cells usually lack chloroplasts.','Every plant cell contains chloroplasts.','Plant cells cannot respire.','Photosynthetic cells need chloroplasts; most root cells do not receive light.')
pack('specialised','Specialised cell=A cell with features suited to a particular function.~Unicellular organism=An organism made of one cell.','Why does a root hair cell have a long extension?','It increases absorption surface area.','It makes the cell an animal cell.','It allows the root to make sunlight.','A larger surface area supports uptake of water and mineral ions.')
pack('diffusion','Diffusion=Net movement of particles from higher to lower concentration due to random motion.~Concentration gradient=A difference in concentration between regions.','What happens at diffusion equilibrium?','Particles still move in both directions.','All particles stop moving.','Particles move only uphill in concentration.','Random motion continues, but there is no overall net transfer between equal concentrations.')
pack('organisation','Tissue=A group of cells working together in a function.~Organ=A structure made of different tissues working together.~Ligament=A structure connecting bone to bone.','Which structure connects muscle to bone?','A tendon.','A ligament.','Cartilage alone.','Tendons transmit a muscle’s pull to a bone; ligaments join bones.')
pack('muscles','Antagonistic pair=Two muscles that pull in opposite directions around a joint.~Contraction=The active development of tension in a muscle.','Why are opposing muscles needed at an elbow?','A contracting muscle pulls rather than actively pushing.','One muscle pushes when it relaxes.','Bones contract to reverse the movement.','One muscle pulls for one direction and the opposing muscle pulls for the reverse movement.')
pack('particles','Solid=A state with particles vibrating about fixed positions.~Liquid=A state with close particles that can move past one another.~Gas=A state with widely separated, freely moving particles.','Why is a gas easy to compress?','There are large gaps between particles.','Its particles shrink under pressure.','It contains no particles.','Compression mainly reduces the gaps between gas particles.')
pack('state','Melting=Changing from solid to liquid.~Evaporation=Escape of particles from a liquid surface into the gas state.~Condensation=Changing from gas to liquid.','How does evaporation differ from boiling?','It can occur at the surface below the boiling point.','It changes water into a new element.','It must occur throughout the liquid.','Boiling occurs throughout a liquid at its boiling point for the stated pressure.')
pack('gas','Gas pressure=Force per unit area caused by particle collisions with surfaces.~Compression=A reduction in volume.','Heating a sealed rigid gas container usually does what?','Increases pressure.','Decreases particle number.','Stops particle collisions.','Faster particles make more frequent and more forceful collisions with the fixed walls.')
pack('atoms','Element=A substance containing one type of atom.~Compound=A substance with different elements chemically bonded in fixed proportions.~Mixture=Substances together without being chemically combined.','Which is a compound?','Pure water.','Air.','Iron.','Water has hydrogen and oxygen chemically combined in a fixed ratio.')
pack('solution','Solute=A substance dissolved in a solvent.~Solvent=A substance that dissolves a solute.~Saturated solution=A solution containing the maximum dissolved solute at the stated conditions.','What does stirring usually increase?','The rate of dissolving.','The maximum solubility at a fixed temperature.','The number of atoms in the salt.','Stirring brings fresh solvent into contact with solute; it need not change the final soluble amount.')
pack('filter','Residue=Material retained by a filter.~Filtrate=Fluid that passes through a filter.~Crystallisation=Formation of solid crystals from a solution.','Can ordinary filter paper separate dissolved salt from water?','No; dissolved salt passes through with the water.','Yes; every salt particle is trapped.','Yes; filtering turns salt into sand.','Filtration separates an insoluble solid, not dissolved particles, from a liquid.')
pack('distil','Distillation=Separation using evaporation followed by condensation.~Chromatography=Separation of components that move differently with a mobile phase.','What does a condenser do in distillation?','Cools vapour back into liquid.','Filters dissolved salt from liquid.','Creates new water molecules.','Condensation collects the evaporated solvent while non-volatile solute stays behind.')
pack('energy','Energy store=A way of accounting for energy held in a system.~Dissipation=Spreading energy into less useful stores, often in the surroundings.','What happens to energy described as “wasted”?','It is transferred into less useful stores.','It is destroyed.','It stops being conserved.','Total energy remains conserved even when useful energy becomes dispersed.')
pack('food-energy','Chemical energy store=Energy associated with the chemical arrangement of substances.~Energy per gram=Energy divided by the mass of material involved.','Why compare food energy per gram?','Food samples may have different masses.','A temperature rise is always the full food energy.','Every food sample has the same energy.','A fair comparison accounts for sample mass and limitations such as heat loss.')
pack('resources','Renewable resource=An energy resource replenished on a human timescale.~Non-renewable resource=A resource used faster than it is naturally replenished.','Which resource is renewable?','Wind.','Natural gas.','Uranium fuel.','Wind is replenished naturally, although the available output varies.')
pack('power','Power=The rate of energy transfer.~Kilowatt-hour=Energy transferred by a power of one kilowatt for one hour.','What quantity is measured in kWh?','Energy.','Power.','Electric current.','Power multiplied by time gives energy; kW alone is a power unit.')
pack('efficiency','Efficiency=Useful output energy or power divided by total input energy or power.~Sankey diagram=A diagram whose arrow widths represent energy transfers.','A device gives 20 J useful output from 100 J input. What is its efficiency?','20%.','80%.','500%.','Efficiency = 20 ÷ 100 × 100% = 20%.')
pack('variation','Variation=Differences between individuals.~Continuous variation=Variation taking values across a measurable range.~Discontinuous variation=Variation in distinct categories.','Which is a suitable continuous variable?','Arm span.','ABO blood group.','The category “present or absent”.','Arm span can take a range of measured values, unlike a discrete category.')
pack('adaptation','Adaptation=An inherited feature that can improve survival and reproduction in an environment.~Selection pressure=An environmental factor affecting reproductive success.','Which explains an inherited adaptation?','It can become more common over generations.','An organism chooses a new gene when needed.','All adaptations help in every environment.','The advantage depends on conditions, and inherited population changes occur across generations.')
pack('puberty','Puberty=Hormone-controlled changes leading to sexual maturity.~Gamete=A reproductive cell carrying one set of chromosomes.','Where are sperm produced?','Testes.','Uterus.','Oviducts.','Testes produce sperm; ovaries produce egg cells.')
pack('cycle','Menstruation=Shedding of the uterine lining.~Ovulation=Release of an egg cell from an ovary.','What is a limitation of a fixed 28-day cycle diagram?','Real cycle length and ovulation timing vary.','Every cycle must last exactly 28 days.','Ovulation and menstruation are identical.','The diagram is a teaching model and does not predict every individual’s timing.')
pack('pregnancy','Fertilisation=Fusion of male and female gamete nuclei.~Implantation=Embedding of an embryo in the uterine lining.~Placenta=An organ allowing exchange between normally separate maternal and fetal blood supplies.','Where does human fertilisation usually happen?','An oviduct.','The uterine lining during implantation.','The placenta.','Fertilisation usually precedes the embryo’s later implantation in the uterus.')
pack('flower','Pollination=Transfer of pollen from anther to stigma.~Anther=The part of a stamen that produces pollen.~Stigma=The pollen-receiving surface of a flower.','Which is pollination?','Pollen reaching a compatible stigma.','Fusion of gamete nuclei.','A seed starting to grow.','Pollination transfers pollen; fertilisation is a later fusion of nuclei.')
pack('seed','Seed dispersal=Movement of seeds away from the parent plant.~Ovule=A structure in the ovary that can develop into a seed after fertilisation.','What commonly develops into a seed?','A fertilised ovule.','The whole anther.','A petal after implantation.','The ovule becomes the seed; the ovary commonly develops into the fruit.')
pack('germination','Germination=The start of seed growth into a seedling.~Control group=A comparison group used to help interpret an investigation.','Which is commonly needed for seed germination?','Water, oxygen and a suitable temperature.','Light for every type of seed.','Soil as the seed’s food supply.','Seeds initially use stored reserves; many germinate without light but need oxygen for respiration.')
pack('reaction','Reactant=A substance present at the start of a chemical reaction.~Product=A substance formed in a chemical reaction.~Physical change=A change that leaves chemical identity unchanged.','Which observation alone does not prove a chemical reaction?','Bubbles appearing.','Evidence that a new substance formed.','A confirmed change in chemical identity.','Boiling also produces bubbles without making a new chemical substance.')
pack('acid','Acid=In school aqueous examples, a substance giving a solution with pH below 7.~Alkali=A base that dissolves in water.~Concentration=Amount of solute per volume of solution.','What does “concentrated” describe?','Amount of solute per solution volume.','How completely an acid ionises.','A guarantee that an acid is strong.','Concentration and acid strength are different properties.')
pack('neutral','Neutralisation=Reaction of an acid with a base.~Salt=A compound formed when the hydrogen of an acid is replaced by metal or ammonium ions.','Which salt name follows from hydrochloric acid?','A chloride.','A sulfate.','A nitrate.','Hydrochloric acid forms chlorides; sulfuric acid forms sulfates.')
pack('metals','Displacement=A reaction in which a more reactive element replaces a less reactive one in a compound.~Oxidation=At this level, a reaction in which a substance gains oxygen.','Can copper displace iron from iron sulfate, if iron is more reactive?','No.','Yes, because all metals displace each other.','Yes, because copper is coloured.','A metal displaces another only when it is more reactive under the stated conditions.')
pack('metal-acid','Reactivity series=An ordering of metals by their tendency to react.~Hydrogen test=A small sample gives a squeaky pop with a lighted splint under supervision.','What forms when magnesium reacts with dilute hydrochloric acid?','Magnesium chloride and hydrogen.','Magnesium sulfate and oxygen.','Only water.','Metal plus a suitable dilute acid forms a salt and hydrogen.')
pack('charge','Electron=A particle carrying negative electric charge.~Static charge=A charge imbalance that can build up on an object.','An insulating rod gains electrons. What is its charge?','Negative.','Positive.','Necessarily neutral.','Gaining negative electrons produces an overall negative charge.')
pack('current','Current=Rate of flow of electric charge.~Ammeter=An instrument measuring current, connected in series.','What happens to current at a junction?','Total current in equals total current out.','Current is used up.','Each branch always has the full supply current.','Charge is conserved, so branch currents add to the current in the shared wire.')
pack('voltage','Potential difference=Energy transferred per unit charge.~Resistance=Potential difference divided by current.~Voltmeter=An instrument connected across a component to measure potential difference.','How is a voltmeter connected?','In parallel across a component.','In series instead of the connecting wire.','Across nothing, with one lead unattached.','Potential difference is measured between two points across a component.')
pack('foodweb','Producer=An organism that makes organic substances, usually by photosynthesis.~Consumer=An organism that obtains organic material by eating other organisms.~Food web=Connected feeding relationships in a community.','Which way does a food-chain arrow point?','From food to the organism eating it.','Always towards the producer.','From predator to prey to show hunting.','The arrow shows the direction of energy and material transfer.')
pack('ecosystem','Ecosystem=A community and its interactions with the non-living environment.~Competition=An interaction over a shared limited resource.~Quadrat=A frame marking a known sampling area.','Why choose quadrat positions randomly?','To reduce selection bias.','To guarantee identical counts.','To find only the most crowded patches.','Random placement avoids deliberately favouring particular parts of the habitat.')
pack('speed','Average speed=Total distance divided by total time.~Repeat=A new measurement under the same chosen conditions.','A trolley travels 6 m in 3 s. Its average speed is what?','2 m/s.','18 m/s.','0.5 m/s.','Divide distance by time: 6 ÷ 3 = 2 m/s.')
pack('dt','Gradient=Change in the vertical quantity divided by change in the horizontal quantity.~Distance–time graph=A graph relating distance to elapsed time.','What does a horizontal distance–time segment show?','The distance stays constant.','The speed keeps increasing.','The road is flat.','A horizontal segment has zero gradient and shows no change in distance.')
pack('force','Resultant force=The overall vector sum of forces on an object.~Balanced forces=Forces with zero resultant.','An object has balanced forces. What can it do?','Remain at rest or move at constant velocity.','Only remain at rest.','Keep increasing its speed.','Zero resultant means velocity does not change, not that velocity must be zero.')
pack('gravity','Mass=The amount of matter in an object, measured in kilograms.~Weight=The gravitational force on an object.~Terminal velocity=Constant velocity reached when forces during a fall balance.','At terminal velocity, how do drag and weight compare?','They are equal and opposite.','Weight disappears.','Drag must be zero.','Their zero resultant means the falling object no longer accelerates.')
pack('crater','Model=A simplified representation used to explore an idea.~Control variable=A factor kept consistent while another is changed.','In a drop-height crater test, what should usually stay the same?','The ball and prepared surface.','The drop height.','The crater diameter measured.','Drop height is changed and crater diameter measured; ball and surface properties should be controlled.')
pack('elements','Chemical symbol=A one- or two-letter label for an element, starting with a capital letter.~Chemical formula=A representation of the elements and atom ratios in a substance.','What does CO₂ represent?','One carbon atom for every two oxygen atoms.','Cobalt with two electrons.','Two carbon atoms for each oxygen atom.','The subscript 2 applies to oxygen; Co and CO have different meanings.')
pack('polymer','Polymer=A substance with very large molecules made from repeating units.~Biodegradable=Capable of being broken down by biological processes under suitable conditions.','Which statement about polymers is accurate?','Different polymers have different properties.','Every polymer is readily biodegradable.','All polymers are the same plastic.','Structure and interactions vary, so properties and disposal options differ.')
pack('periodic','Group=A vertical column in the periodic table.~Period=A horizontal row in the periodic table.','What is a useful feature of elements in a group?','They often have related chemical properties.','They must have identical melting points.','They are all the same element.','A group gives patterns that support predictions, while individual properties still differ.')
pack('groups','Halogen=A reactive non-metal in school Group 7, modern Group 17.~Noble gas=An element in school Group 0, modern Group 18, with very low ordinary reactivity.','How does Group 1 reactivity generally change down the group?','It increases.','It decreases like the halogens.','It stays exactly identical.','Alkali-metal reactivity generally increases down Group 1; halogen reactivity generally decreases.')
pack('transition','Catalyst=A substance that speeds a reaction and is regenerated overall.~Transition metal=One of the central metals commonly showing properties such as coloured compounds and catalytic activity.','Why is copper useful in electrical wiring?','It conducts electricity well.','It is an electrical insulator.','Every copper compound is an identical conductor.','A material’s properties must be linked to the job; copper metal is a good conductor.')
pack('lungs','Ventilation=Movement of air into and out of the lungs.~Alveolus=A small air sac providing a gas-exchange surface.~Diaphragm=A sheet of muscle helping change chest volume.','What happens during normal inhalation?','The diaphragm contracts and chest volume increases.','The diaphragm relaxes and chest volume decreases.','Oxygen is made by the lungs.','Greater chest volume lowers pressure, allowing air to enter.')
pack('drugs','Drug=A substance that changes how the body works.~Dependence=A state in which stopping a substance can be difficult and may cause withdrawal effects.','Which statement about nicotine vaping is accurate?','It is not risk-free and is not suitable for children or non-smokers.','It is harmless because tobacco is not burned.','Nicotine cannot cause dependence.','Lower harm than smoking does not mean no risk; nicotine is addictive.')
pack('nutrition','Nutrient=A substance needed for growth, maintenance or normal body processes.~Deficiency=A shortage of a required nutrient.~Fibre=Dietary material that supports digestive function and is not digested by human enzymes.','What is a role of iron in the diet?','Making haemoglobin.','Providing most dietary energy.','Replacing the need for water.','Haemoglobin needs iron and supports oxygen transport.')
pack('food-tests','Positive test=A result indicating the tested substance was detected under those conditions.~Reducing sugar=A sugar detected by Benedict’s reagent with suitable heating.','What is a positive starch test?','Iodine turns blue-black.','Benedict’s stays blue.','Biuret turns orange.','Iodine is used for starch; protein and reducing sugars need different tests.')
pack('digestion','Digestion=Breaking large insoluble food molecules into smaller soluble ones.~Enzyme=A biological catalyst.~Absorption=Transfer of digested substances across the gut wall.','How do villi help absorption?','They provide a large surface area.','They make food molecules larger.','They stop blood reaching the intestine.','A large area, thin surface and blood supply support exchange of digested nutrients.')
pack('sound','Amplitude=Maximum displacement from an undisturbed position.~Frequency=Number of complete oscillations per second.~Longitudinal wave=A wave with oscillations parallel to energy transfer.','What usually changes when a sound has a higher pitch?','Frequency increases.','Amplitude alone increases.','It begins to travel through a vacuum.','Pitch relates to frequency; amplitude relates to intensity and perceived loudness.')
pack('ear','Echo=A reflected sound.~Cochlea=An inner-ear structure with receptors converting vibrations into nerve signals.','Why divide speed × echo time by two to find a wall’s distance?','The sound travels to the wall and back.','Sound always travels at half speed.','The wall absorbs all sound.','The measured time covers a return path, twice the direct distance to the reflector.')
pack('light','Normal=A line perpendicular to a surface at the ray’s point of contact.~Reflection=Return of a wave from a boundary.~Refraction=Change in wave direction due to a change in speed across a boundary.','From where are reflection angles measured?','The normal.','The mirror surface.','The end of the ruler.','The normal is perpendicular to the surface and is the reference for incidence and reflection angles.')
pack('colour','Filter=A material transmitting some wavelengths while absorbing others.~Convex lens=A lens that can converge parallel light rays.~Spectrum=A range of radiation arranged by wavelength or frequency.','Why may a red object look dark in blue light?','It reflects little of the available blue light.','It makes its own red light.','The blue light has no energy.','A red object mainly reflects red wavelengths, which are missing from pure blue illumination.')
pack('combustion','Thermal decomposition=Breaking down a compound by heating.~Complete combustion=Reaction of a fuel with sufficient oxygen to form fully oxidised products.','Which is thermal decomposition?','A compound breaking into simpler substances on heating.','Ice melting into water.','A solute being filtered out of a solution.','Decomposition forms different substances; melting changes only physical state.')
pack('mass','Closed system=A system with no matter entering or leaving.~Conservation of mass=Total mass stays constant in an ordinary chemical reaction when all matter is counted.','Why can an open reaction flask lose mass?','A gas product leaves the measured flask.','Atoms are destroyed.','The balance converts matter into nothing.','The gas still exists outside the flask; the complete system conserves mass.')
pack('chemical-energy','Exothermic=A process transferring energy to the surroundings overall.~Endothermic=A process taking energy from the surroundings overall.~Bond=A chemical link between atoms.','Which statement about bonds is correct?','Breaking bonds requires energy.','Breaking bonds always releases energy.','Forming bonds always requires energy overall.','Bond formation releases energy; the balance between breaking and forming determines the overall transfer.')
pack('catalyst','Activation energy=The energy barrier needed to start a reaction.~Catalyst=A substance that provides a faster reaction pathway and is regenerated overall.','What can a catalyst change for the same reactant amount?','Time taken to reach the final gas volume.','The total number of atoms.','Always the final amount of gas.','A catalyst changes rate, so completion can occur sooner without changing the final product amount.')
pack('respiration','Aerobic respiration=Cellular breakdown of glucose using oxygen to transfer energy.~Respiration=A set of cellular chemical processes transferring energy from organic substances.','Which organisms respire?','Plants and animals.','Only animals.','Plants only at night.','Plant and animal cells need energy transfer continually; photosynthesis does not replace respiration.')
pack('anaerobic','Anaerobic respiration=Energy transfer from glucose without using oxygen.~Fermentation=Anaerobic respiration in yeast producing ethanol and carbon dioxide.','Which gas helps bread dough rise?','Carbon dioxide from yeast.','Oxygen made by yeast fermentation.','Nitrogen from glucose.','Yeast fermentation produces carbon dioxide, whose bubbles expand the dough.')
pack('photosynthesis','Photosynthesis=Using light to make glucose from carbon dioxide and water, releasing oxygen.~Chlorophyll=A pigment that absorbs light for photosynthesis.','Where does much of a plant’s dry carbon come from?','Carbon dioxide in the air.','Mineral ions as a source of carbon.','Soil as ready-made plant food.','Photosynthesis incorporates carbon from carbon dioxide into organic material.')
pack('leaf','Stoma=A pore in a leaf surface that allows gas exchange.~Limiting factor=A factor preventing the rate from increasing under the current conditions.','Why can extra light eventually stop increasing photosynthesis?','Another factor becomes limiting.','Plants stop needing carbon dioxide.','All chloroplasts disappear.','If carbon dioxide or temperature limits the rate, more light alone does not remove the limit.')
pack('plant-practical','Proxy=A measurement used as an indicator of another quantity.~Destarching=Allowing stored starch in a plant to be used up before an investigation.','Why can oxygen volume be better than bubble count?','Bubbles can have different volumes.','Every bubble always has the same size.','Volume removes all biological variation.','Counting bubbles treats unequal bubbles as equal; a collected volume measures gas more directly.')
pack('investigate-candle','Independent variable=The factor deliberately changed.~Endpoint=A clearly defined event used to finish a measurement.','What should be controlled in an air-volume candle investigation?','Candle and wick conditions.','The container volume being tested.','The time to extinction being measured.','Control the fuel and starting conditions so container volume is the intended difference.')
pack('investigate-bounce','Rebound height=The maximum height reached after a bounce.~Dissipation=Transfer of energy into less useful stores in the surroundings.','Why is a rebound usually lower than the drop height?','Some mechanical energy is dissipated.','Energy is destroyed at impact.','Gravity switches off after the bounce.','Energy transfers to thermal stores and sound, so less remains for the rebound.')
pack('investigate-bungee','Extension=Stretched length minus original length.~Elastic deformation=A change of shape reversed when the force is removed.','A cord lengthens from 20 cm to 28 cm. What is its extension?','8 cm.','28 cm.','48 cm.','Extension measures the increase in length, not the full stretched length.')
pack('investigate-conduction','Thermal conductor=A material through which thermal energy transfers readily.~Fair comparison=A comparison controlling other factors that could affect the result.','What is essential when comparing metal rods?','Matching dimensions and heating conditions.','A different flame for each rod.','Changing rod length together with metal type.','Geometry and heating affect transfer, so uncontrolled changes could explain the result.')
pack('investigate-cupcake','Drag=A force opposing motion through a fluid.~Terminal velocity=Constant velocity when weight and resistive forces balance.','Does one average falling speed prove terminal velocity was reached?','No; it can combine changing speeds.','Yes; every falling object is always at terminal velocity.','Yes; time measurements show forces are zero.','Evidence of a constant speed over part of the fall is needed.')
pack('investigate-salt','Solubility=Maximum amount dissolving in a given solvent amount at stated conditions.~Dissolving rate=How quickly a solute dissolves.','What happens if too much salt is added to a saturated solution?','Some remains undissolved.','It must all dissolve given the same conditions.','The salt particles stop existing.','At a fixed temperature and solvent amount, the maximum soluble amount has been reached.')
pack('investigate-diffusion','Diffusion=Net movement down a concentration gradient due to random motion.~Convection=Energy and material transfer by bulk movement in a fluid.','What can confound a warm-water dye-spreading experiment?','Convection currents.','Keeping dye amount the same.','Using the same vessel shape.','Bulk movement can carry dye, so visible spreading is not necessarily due to diffusion alone.')
pack('magnet','Magnetic field=A region where a magnet or magnetic material experiences a force.~Induced magnet=A material becoming magnetised in a nearby magnetic field.','Which is stronger evidence that an object is itself a magnet?','It repels a known magnet in one orientation.','It is attracted to a magnet.','It is shiny.','Induced magnetic materials can be attracted; repulsion indicates interacting magnetic poles.')
pack('electromagnet','Electromagnet=A magnet produced by current in a coil.~Relay=A switch operated by an electromagnet.','How can an electromagnet be strengthened?','Increase the current under suitable safe conditions.','Remove every coil turn.','Switch the current off.','A larger coil current increases its magnetic field; turns and core also matter.')
pack('work','Work done=Energy transferred by a force moving an object through a distance in its direction.~Simple machine=A device trading force against movement distance.','An ideal pulley halves the lifting force. What is the trade-off?','Twice the input pulling distance for the same lifted height.','Free extra energy.','Half the input distance too.','Input work equals useful output work in the ideal model, so force × distance stays consistent.')
pack('moments','Moment=The turning effect of a force about a pivot.~Pivot=The point or axis about which turning occurs.','Which distance is used to calculate a moment?','Perpendicular distance to the force’s line of action.','Any diagonal distance to the load.','The total length of the object.','The effective lever arm is perpendicular to the line of action.')
pack('thermal','Temperature=A measure related to average particle kinetic energy.~Conduction=Thermal energy transfer through interactions without bulk flow.','Does a hotter small cup necessarily have a larger thermal store than a cooler bath?','No; mass and material also matter.','Yes; temperature alone decides the total store.','Yes; water has no mass.','Total thermal energy depends on the amount of material as well as temperature.')
pack('convection','Convection=Transfer by bulk movement of a fluid.~Thermal radiation=Energy transfer by electromagnetic radiation.~Insulator=A material or arrangement that reduces a transfer pathway.','Which transfer can cross a vacuum?','Thermal radiation.','Convection across the vacuum.','Conduction by absent particles.','Electromagnetic radiation needs no material medium; conduction and convection do.')
pack('atmosphere','Atmosphere=The gases surrounding a planet.~Carbon cycle=Movement of carbon through organisms and the physical environment.','Which process removes carbon dioxide from air?','Photosynthesis.','Burning fossil fuels.','Aerobic respiration.','Photosynthesis incorporates carbon into organic substances; combustion and respiration return it.')
pack('climate','Greenhouse gas=A gas absorbing and re-emitting infrared radiation.~Climate=Patterns in weather over long periods.','What is central to the enhanced greenhouse effect?','Changed absorption and re-emission of outgoing infrared.','A hole allowing extra visible sunlight through.','One cold day disproving a long-term trend.','Increasing greenhouse gases changes Earth’s energy balance and contributes to warming.')
pack('extraction','Ore=A material containing enough useful substance for extraction to be worthwhile.~Reduction=Here, removal of oxygen from a metal oxide.','Why is aluminium not normally extracted using carbon reduction?','Aluminium is more reactive than carbon.','Aluminium contains no atoms.','Carbon can extract every metal.','Carbon reduces oxides of suitable metals below it in the reactivity series.')
pack('recycle','Reuse=Using a product again without converting its material into a new material product.~Recycling=Processing recovered materials into new products.~Life cycle=Stages from extraction through production and use to disposal.','Which is a sound recycling evaluation?','Compare resources and energy across the life cycle.','Assume recycling needs no energy.','Ignore collection and contamination.','Benefits can be substantial, but processing, collection and sorting still have impacts.')
pack('selection','Natural selection=Change in inherited trait frequencies through differences in survival and reproduction.~Inherited variation=Differences linked to genetic information passed between generations.','Which is needed for natural selection to change a population?','Inherited differences affecting reproduction.','Individuals choosing which mutations they need.','Every individual surviving equally with no inherited variation.','Differences must connect to inheritance and reproductive success across generations.')
pack('evidence-evolution','Scientific theory=A tested explanatory framework supported by evidence.~Acquired characteristic=A feature developed during an individual’s lifetime.','Why is muscle training not itself inherited evolution?','An acquired change need not alter inherited genetic information.','Every acquired trait is automatically inherited.','Evolution means any individual change.','Evolution concerns inherited population changes across generations.')
pack('biodiversity','Biodiversity=The variety of life, including species and genetic diversity.~Extinction=Loss of all living members of a species.~Conservation=Actions protecting species, habitats and biodiversity.','Why may captive breeding alone fail to restore a species?','Suitable wild habitat and reduced threats are also needed.','Breeding automatically removes every threat.','Genetic diversity never matters.','A self-sustaining wild population needs conditions supporting survival and reproduction.')
pack('inheritance','Gene=A section of DNA contributing to a functional product.~Allele=An alternative version of a gene.~Chromosome=A long DNA molecule associated with proteins.','What is an allele?','A version of a gene.','An entire organism.','A synonym for every chromosome.','Different versions of a gene can contribute to inherited variation.')
pack('dna-practical','DNA=The molecule carrying genetic information.~Precipitate=A solid that separates from a solution.','What do visible strands in a classroom DNA extraction show?','DNA-rich material from many molecules, potentially with impurities.','The exact base sequence by eye.','A complete view of one individual gene.','The extraction makes aggregated material visible; it does not resolve the molecular sequence.')
pack('cross','Genotype=The allele combination for a gene or genes.~Phenotype=An expressed characteristic.~Recessive allele=An allele whose phenotype in a simple complete-dominance model needs two copies.','For Aa × Aa in a simple complete-dominance model, what is P(aa)?','1/4.','Exactly one in every four siblings.','3/4.','Each offspring has an independent one-in-four chance; a family need not match the ratio exactly.')
pack('gm','Genetic modification=Changing genetic material using biotechnology.~Selective breeding=Choosing parents with desired inherited traits over generations.','How does selective breeding differ from genetic modification?','It chooses parents rather than directly editing genetic material.','It changes every individual instantly.','It cannot affect inherited characteristics.','Selecting parents changes inherited combinations over generations, using a different method from direct modification.')
pack('waves','Transverse wave=A wave with oscillations perpendicular to energy transfer.~Wavelength=Spacing between corresponding points on a wave.~Period=Time for one complete oscillation.','A wave has f=3 Hz and wavelength 2 m. What is its speed?','6 m/s.','1.5 m/s.','5 m/s.','Wave speed = frequency × wavelength = 3 × 2 = 6 m/s.')
pack('ultrasound','Ultrasound=Sound above about 20 kHz.~Time of flight=The travel time of a signal over a path.','Can ultrasound cross a vacuum?','No; it needs a material medium.','Yes; it is an electromagnetic wave.','Yes; it is ionising radiation.','Ultrasound is a mechanical sound wave, despite its high frequency.')
pack('em','Electromagnetic wave=A wave that can transfer energy through a vacuum.~Ionisation=Removal or addition of electrons to form charged particles.','Which EM waves have the same speed in a vacuum?','All of them.','Only visible colours.','Only waves with the same frequency.','Their vacuum speed is the same; frequency and wavelength differ.')
pack('earth','Crust=The thin outer solid layer of Earth.~Mantle=The mostly solid rock layer beneath the crust, able to deform slowly.~Igneous rock=Rock formed when molten material cools and solidifies.','Which description of Earth is accurate?','The mantle is mostly solid and the outer core is liquid.','The entire mantle is a liquid magma ocean.','The crust is Earth’s thickest layer.','The mantle can flow slowly over geological time without being a global liquid ocean.')
pack('sediment','Weathering=Breakdown of rock in place.~Erosion=Movement of rock material.~Fossil=Preserved evidence of a former organism.','How does erosion differ from weathering?','Erosion transports material.','Erosion must form magma.','Weathering always moves sediment downstream.','Weathering breaks material down where it is; erosion moves it.')
pack('rock-cycle','Metamorphic rock=Existing rock changed by heat and pressure without melting.~Rock cycle=Connected processes transforming rocks through multiple pathways.','What forms when rock melts and then solidifies?','Igneous rock.','Metamorphic rock without melting.','The same rock unchanged.','Cooling molten material forms igneous rock; metamorphism does not require melting.')
pack('space','Light-year=The distance light travels in one year.~Solar System=The Sun and the bodies gravitationally associated with it.~Galaxy=A large system of stars, gas and other matter.','What does a light-year measure?','Distance.','Time.','Brightness.','The word “year” describes the travel interval used to define a very large distance.')
pack('orbits','Orbit=The path of a body moving around another under gravity.~Axial tilt=The angle of a body’s rotation axis relative to its orbital orientation.','What mainly causes Earth’s seasons?','Axial tilt changing sunlight angle and day length.','A large annual change in Earth–Sun distance.','The Moon blocking sunlight every winter.','The hemispheres receive different sunlight angles and day lengths as tilted Earth orbits.')
pack('moon','Phase=The visible fraction of the Moon’s illuminated half.~Lunar eclipse=The Moon passing through Earth’s shadow.~Solar eclipse=The Moon’s shadow falling on Earth.','What causes ordinary Moon phases?','Our changing view of its sunlit half.','Earth’s shadow every night.','The Moon switching its own light on and off.','Phases arise from viewing geometry; Earth’s shadow is involved in a lunar eclipse.')
pack('friction','Friction=A force opposing relative sliding or its tendency at a contact.~Drag=A force opposing motion through a fluid.~Plastic deformation=A permanent change of shape.','Why does a cyclist need a driving force at steady speed?','To balance friction and drag.','To create a forward resultant continually.','Because balanced forces mean no movement.','At steady velocity, driving and resistive forces balance rather than vanish.')
pack('spring','Spring constant=Force divided by extension in a proportional spring region.~Limit of proportionality=The point beyond which force is no longer proportional to extension.','What length belongs in F=kx?','Extension, not total spring length.','The original spring length.','Any measured length of the apparatus.','Extension is stretched length minus original length, in units consistent with k.')
pack('pressure','Pressure=Perpendicular force per unit area.~Pascal=One newton per square metre.','At the same force, what does doubling contact area do?','Halves pressure.','Doubles pressure.','Leaves pressure unchanged.','Pressure = force ÷ area, so it falls when the same force is spread over more area.')
pack('liquid-pressure','Hydraulic system=A system transmitting a pressure change through an enclosed fluid.~Liquid pressure=Force per unit area exerted by a liquid.','Why can a hydraulic output piston provide a larger force?','The same pressure acts over a larger area.','The fluid creates extra energy.','The larger piston always moves farther too.','F=pA; in the ideal system a larger force trades against a smaller movement distance.')
